## Synthesis and spectroscopic properties of \(\mathsf{K}_{x}\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{B D P})_{3}\)
Solid \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) can be reduced with potassium naphthalenide in tetrahydrofuran (THF) to obtain \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) \((0<x\leq2.0)\) (Fig. 1c), and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data revealed that the reaction is topotactic for all levels of reduction (Fig. 2a). Partial filling of the pores with solvated potassium ions results in a decrease in framework surface area with successive reduction, although \(\mathrm{K}_{0.9}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{K}_{1.1}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) still exhibit permanent porosity (BET surface areas of 610 and \(430\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, Fig. 2b).
The ultraviolet–visible–near-infrared diffuse reflectance spectrum of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) exhibits a broad absorption band (Fig. 2c) that is
maintained through reduction to \(\mathrm{K}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\). In the case of \(\mathrm{K}_{0.9}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\), the absorption band above \(1.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) appears strongly diminished (Fig. 2c). A band edge returns for \(1.1\!\le\!x\!\le\!2.0\), with band gaps between 1.40 and \(1.49\,\mathrm{eV}\). At approximately half reduction, \(\mathrm{KFe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\), we might expect the greatest degree of valence delocalization, and indeed a strong, sharp absorption band is observed between 0.5 and \(1.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{K}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\). This feature is most prominent on reduction to \(\mathrm{K}_{0.9}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\), with a peak energy of \(0.52\,\mathrm{eV}\), and diminishes as \(x\) approaches 2.0. As this feature emerges only at intermediate levels of reduction, it was assigned as an intervalence charge-transfer band—suggesting the formation of mid-gap polarons and possibly enhanced charge transport properties. Notably, absorption bands of similar energies have been assigned to intervalence charge-transfer transitions in conductive, mixed-valence charge transfer salts and metal complexes\(^{25,27}\). With exposure to air, \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) gradually oxidizes to yield an absorption spectrum very similar to that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\).
On initial reduction, mid-infrared spectroscopy revealed the emergence of a broad absorption band from \(2,500{-}450\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). The intensity and width of this feature increase until \(\mathrm{K}_{0.9}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\), and then decrease on further reduction (Fig. 2d). Similar mid-infrared bands have been observed for conductive mixed-valence polymers of pyrazine-bridged iron(II), ruthenium(II) and osmium(II) porphyrinates\(^{30}\), mixed-valence charge transfer salts\(^{31}\) and doped polyacetylene\(^{32}\), and have been attributed to low-energy transitions between delocalized states.
