## Conductivity and charge mobility in bulk \(\mathsf{K}_{x}\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{B D P})_{3}\)
Flash photolysistime-resolvedmicrowaveconductivity(FP-TRMC)37,38 measurements were used to gain insight into the intrinsic charge transport characteristics of \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) . The phase \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) itself is intrinsically conductive, with a charge mobility \((\mu_{\mathrm{tot}})\) of \(0.02\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , and on reduction the charge mobility increases to a maximum value of \(0.29\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{K}_{0.8}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) . The conductivity transients and calculated charge mobilities for \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) \((x\!=\!0,\,0.2,\,0.4,\) and 0.8) are shown in Fig. 5a, and all measured mobility values are comparable to those of common organic conductors such as rubrene \((0.05\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) \(\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})^{39}\) and poly \(\boldsymbol{p}\) -phenylene vinylene) \((0.1\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})^{40}\) .
With few exceptions41, measurements of electronic conductivity in MOFs have been limited to pressed pellet studies and analysis of polycrystalline thin films16,17,42. However, such measurements often probe only interparticle contacts, precluding a fundamental understanding of charge transport. Contactless microwave conductivity measurements37 can provide a good probe of intrinsic charge transport, although they cannot directly yield the sign of the charge carriers. In contrast, single-crystal field-effect transistor (FET) measurements enable determination of both the mobility and charge carrier sign, while the devices can be fabricated to resemble functional architectures. To date, FET studies on metal–organic hybrid materials have been largely limited to thin films, such as lead halide perovskites and \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{C}}_{6}{\mathrm{S}}_{6}\) (ref. 1), and no single-crystal FET studies have been carried out on MOFs. However, the high mobilities determined for \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) by FP-TRMC suggested that single-crystal FET studies could yield a better estimate of the transport properties of these materials.
To achieve controlled reduction of the single-crystal devices, the fabricated devices were placed in a vessel under an inert atmosphere containing bulk \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) powder and THF and reduced via addition of potassium naphthalenide in THF. In this way, the bulk \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) and the microcrystals bonded to the microelectrode array were fractionally reduced stepwise and the transport properties of the FET devices were measured after each reduction.
These measured mobilities give further insight into the degree of electronic delocalization in \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\). With an Fe–Fe distance of \(3.58\,\mathrm{\AA}\) and assuming a typical electron–lattice scattering time of \(\tau\!=\!10^{-14}\,\mathrm{s}\), one can apply time-energy uncertainty to estimate that a minimum mobility of about \(0.3\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) is necessary for an effective mass model to be considered45. The measured average FET mobilities (Fig. 5d) and the FP-TRMC mobility, \(\mu_{\mathrm{tot}}\), of \(\mathrm{K}_{0.80}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) of \(0.29\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) suggest that the conduction electrons for \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) with \(0.78\!\le\!x\!<\!2\) can be treated as delocalized along the iron–pyrazolate chains at room temperature. Although no four-contact single-crystal devices survived the repeated reduction process, two-contact d.c. conductivity measurements performed on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) single crystals revealed about a 10,000-fold conductivity increase on reduction to \(\mathrm{K}_{0.98}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\); multiplying the four-contact d.c. conductivity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) by the value yielding an estimation of the four-contact conductivity of a \(\mathrm{K}_{0.98}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) crystal on the order of \(\sim\!7\!\times\!10^{2}\,\mathrm{S\,cm^{-1}}\). The relation \(\sigma\!=\!1\!-\!\mu_{\mathrm{e}}\varepsilon\) then yields a charge
## Outlook
The path towards engendering long-range charge delocalization through the atomically thin pore walls of MOFs remains a challenging endeavor. However, the control and precision that can be achieved through quantitative redox doping and single-crystal FET measurements prove these tools to be invaluable for maximizing conductivity and understanding the underlying electronic structures of this relatively new class of materials. Here we found, by considering the structural motif \([\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{pz})_{3}]_{\infty}\) as an ideal precursor to a conductive mixed-valence material, that sub-stoichiometric reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) yielded both high charge mobilities for a porous conductor and the onset of carrier itineracy at ambient temperatures. Further, the modular \([\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{pz})_{3}]_{\infty}\) building unit may be more easily incorporated into new frameworks of differing topology and surface chemistry than the subunits of some of the most conductive MOF structure types reported46. Indeed, only MOFs such as \(\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}\) with open, three-dimensional networks can fully
realize the synthetic control and diversity of properties that conductive coordination solids have to offer. More broadly, the installation of a repeat mixed-valence substructure is an intuitive and viable mode for building tunable, porous conductor-based devices from well-established models of charge delocalization in coordination chemistry12. By extending the frontier states across multiple repeat units along at least one framework dimension through metal–ligand hybridization, band dispersion can be increased significantly. In doing so, porous crystals may approach the interface between localized—molecule-like—interactions and the broad band dispersion found in classical conductors, all while retaining the porosity, surface chemistry and host–guest chemistry for which MOFs are unique.
